<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ch01-01-Vectors2DIntroduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Vectors in 2D</title>

  <definition>
    <title>Vectors</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A vector is an object defined by
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              its magnitude (length)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              and its direction.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        They are geometrically represented by rays/arrows and symbolically with
        a lowercase letter with an arrow above it or in bold face (such as
        <m>\vec{u}</m> or <m>\mathbf{u}, \vec{v}</m> or <m>\mathbf{v}</m>,
        etc.).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    We will be drawing 2D vectors in the <m>x y</m>-plane (Cartesian plane). In
    this setting, we can define a vector by stating its starting point
    <m>P\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)</m> (arrow tail) and ending point
    <m>Q\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\right)</m> (arrow head).
  </p>

  <p>
    When a vector is given by its starting point <m>P</m> and ending point
    <m>Q</m>, we can represent the vector symbolically using
    <m>\overrightarrow{P Q}</m>.
  </p>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Draw the vectors below with given starting and ending points:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) <m>P(0,0)</m> and <m>Q(3,2)</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) <m>R(0.5,2)</m> and <m>S(3.5,4)</m>
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 1.1.png">
        <shortdescription>Two vectors drawn in the xy-plane: PQ from (0,0) to (3,2) and RS from (0.5,2) to (3.5,4).</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        What can we say about the vectors <m>\overrightarrow{P Q}</m> and
        <m>\overrightarrow{R S}</m>?
      </p>

      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Moving from <m>P(0,0)</m> to <m>Q(3,2)</m> we travel 3 units in the
        <m>x</m> direction and 2 units in the <m>y</m> direction. Moving from
        <m>R(0.5,2)</m> to <m>S(3.5,4)</m> we travel <m>3.5-0.5 = 3</m> units in
        the <m>x</m> direction and <m>4-2 = 2</m> units in the <m>y</m>
        direction. Since the two arrows describe the same displacement, they
        have the same magnitude and the same direction; the only difference
        between them is where they start.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Equivalent Vectors</title>

    <p>
      Vectors are said to be equivalent vectors if they have the same magnitude
      and direction (independent of their starting and ending points).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Vectors in Standard Position</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        If the starting point of a vector is the origin (the point <m>(0,0)</m>
        ), we say the vector is in standard position.
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in standard position, if its ending
        point is ( <m>v_{1}, v_{2}</m> ), we write down the vector
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m> as
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}\right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        and we say that <m>v_{1}</m> is the <m>x</m>-component of
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>v_{2}</m> is the <m>y</m>-component of
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
 <remark>
    <p>
      [Writing a Vector in Standard Position]
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose a vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> has starting point
      <m>P\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)</m> and ending point
      <m>Q\left(x_{2}, y_{2}\right)</m>. The ending point of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>,
      in standard position, is <m>\left(x_{2}-x_{1}, y_{2}-y_{1}\right)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      In other words,
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}=\left\langle x_{2}-x_{1}, y_{2}-y_{1}\right\rangle
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Writing a vector in standard position simply shifts the vector so that its
      starting point is the origin.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Graph the vector with starting point <m>P(2,1)</m> and ending point
        <m>Q(-1,0)</m>; write the vector in standard position; and then graph
        the vector in standard position.
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 1.2.png">
       <shortdescription> A vector from P(2,1) to Q(-1,0) and the same vector in standard position from (0,0) to (-3,-1).</shortdescription> 
      </image>
      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Both graphs are shown above. Using the formula, the ending point of the
        vector in standard position is
        <m>\left(x_{2}-x_{1}, y_{2}-y_{1}\right)</m>, so
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}= \langle -1-2, 0-1 \rangle = \langle -3,-1 \rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        This is the arrow starting at the origin and ending at <m>(-3,-1)</m>:
        the original vector shifted so that its starting point is the origin.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <definition>
    <title>Magnitude of a Vector</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        To find the magnitude/length of a vector:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Put the vector in standard position.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              If the vector is given by
              <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}\right\rangle</m>, then its
              magnitude, denoted by |v|, is given by
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          |\mathbf{v}|=\sqrt{v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the length of the vector with starting point <m>P(1,-2)</m> and
        ending point <m>Q(4,2)</m>. Graph this vector (in standard position) and
        verify that the formula for length is the Pythagorean Theorem.
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 1.3.png">
       <shortdescription> The vector from P(1,-2) to Q(4,2). In standard position, forms a right triangle with legs 3 and 4 and hypotenuse 5.</shortdescription> 
      </image>      
      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        In standard position the vector is
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}= \langle 4-1, 2-(-2) \rangle = \langle 3,4 \rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        so its length is
      </p>
      <md>
        |\mathbf{v}| = \sqrt{3^{2}+4^{2}}= \sqrt{9+16}= \sqrt{25}= 5
      </md>

      <p>
        As the graph above shows, in standard position the vector is the
        hypotenuse of a right triangle whose horizontal leg has length 3 (the
        <m>x</m>-component) and whose vertical leg has length 4 (the
        <m>y</m>-component). The magnitude formula squares the two legs, adds
        them, and takes the square root, which is exactly the Pythagorean
        Theorem: <m>3^{2}+4^{2} = 5^{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

 

  <definition>
    <title>Direction of a Vector</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        To find the direction of a vector:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Put the vector in standard position.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              If the vector is given by
              <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}\right\rangle</m>, its
              direction is the angle <m>\theta</m> starting on the positive x
              -axis and measured counter-clockwise ending on the vector
              <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}\right\rangle</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle-2,-4\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 1.4.png">
       <shortdescription> The vector v = (-2,-4) in standard position in the third quadrant, with the angle theta measured counter-clockwise from the positive x-axis. The angle phi is the portion of theta past the negative x-axis.</shortdescription> 
      </image>        
      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Both components of <m>\mathbf{v}= \langle -2,-4 \rangle</m> are
        negative, so the vector lies in the third quadrant and its direction
        angle satisfies <m>\pi &lt; \theta &lt; \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>. The angle
        <m>\phi</m> that <m>\mathbf{v}</m> makes with the negative <m>x</m>-axis
        satisfies
      </p>
      <md>
        \tan(\phi) = \frac{4}{2}= 2 \Rightarrow \phi = \arctan(2)
      </md>

      <p>
        Hence, measuring counter-clockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis,
      </p>
      <md>
        \theta = \pi+\arctan(2) \approx 4.25 \text{ radians}\approx 243.43^{\circ}
      </md>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The zero vector <m>\mathbf{0}=\langle 0,0\rangle</m> is the only vector
      with zero magnitude and no direction.
    </p>
  </remark>
  <!--\section*{Vector Addition/Subtraction}-->
  <note>
    <p>
      From now on, any time a vector is mentioned, assume it is in standard
      position (unless stated otherwise).
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      [Adding and Subtracting Vectors]
    </p>

    <p>
      To add/subtract two vectors <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, you
      add/subtract the corresponding <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> components.
    </p>
    <!--$$ $$\vspace{&#x2D;.5cm}-->
    <p>
      The result of adding/subtracting two vectors is again a vector.
    </p>

    <p/>
    <!--$$ $$-->
    <p>
      Important: Vector subtraction is not commutative. Hence,
      <m>\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{u}\neq \mathbf{u}-\mathbf{v}</m> (unless
      <m>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{u}</m> ).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{u}</m>
        where <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 3,-2\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle 5,-1\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Adding and subtracting the corresponding components:
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}= \langle 3+5, -2+(-1) \rangle = \langle 8,-3 \rangle \\  \mathbf{v}-\mathbf{u}= \langle 3-5, -2-(-1) \rangle = \langle -2,-1 \rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        Note that both results are again vectors, and that computing
        <m>\mathbf{u}-\mathbf{v}= \langle 2,1 \rangle</m> instead would give a
        different (opposite) vector.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

 

  <note>
    <p>
      There is a geometric interpretation for vector addition/subtraction. We
      can see it using the Triangle Rule.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Triangle Rule states that in order to compute
      <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in standard position.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Starting at the endpoint of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, draw the vector
            <m>\mathbf{u}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Draw a vector starting at <m>(0,0)</m> and ending at the end of the
            vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m> as drawn in step 2.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 1,1\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{u}=\langle 1,-2\rangle</m>, graph
        <m>\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}</m> using the Triangle rule.
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 1.6.png">
       <shortdescription> The Triangle Rule for adding the vector v with coordinates x = 1, y = 1 and u with coordinates x = 1, y = -2: v is drawn from the origin, u is drawn from the tip of v, and the sum v+u is drawn from the origin to the tip of u.</shortdescription> 
      </image>        
      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Following the Triangle Rule, we first draw <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in standard
        position, ending at <m>(1,1)</m>. Starting at that endpoint we draw
        <m>\mathbf{u}</m>, which takes us 1 unit right and 2 units down, ending
        at <m>(1+1, 1-2) = (2,-1)</m>. Finally we draw the vector starting at
        <m>(0,0)</m> and ending at <m>(2,-1)</m>, which is the sum
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{u}= \langle 2,-1 \rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        in agreement with adding the vectors component-wise, as the graph above
        shows.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>


  <p>
    In order to graph <m>\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{u}</m> using the Triangle Rule, draw
    it as <m>\mathbf{v}+(-\mathbf{u})</m>. We define the meaning of
    <m>-\mathbf{u}</m> next.
  </p>

  <remark>
    <title>Multiplying a Vector by a Scalar (Number)</title>

    <p>
      To multiply a vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by a scalar (number) <m>c</m>, you
      multiply the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> components of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by
      <m>c</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The result of multiplying a vector by a scalar is again a vector denoted
      by <m>c \mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the vector <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle-1,1\rangle</m>, compute:
      </p>

      <p>
        (a) <m>4 \mathbf{v}</m>
      </p>

      <p>
        (b) <m>-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{v}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Multiplying each component of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by 4:
      </p>
      <md>
        4\mathbf{v}= \langle 4(-1), 4(1) \rangle = \langle -4,4 \rangle
      </md>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Multiplying each component of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m>:
      </p>
      <md>
        -\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{v}= \left\langle -\frac{1}{2}(-1), -\frac{1}{2}(1) \right\rangle = \left\langle \frac{1}{2},-\frac{1}{2}\right\rangle
      </md>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      There is a geometric interpretation for multiplying a vector
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m> by a number <m>c</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      (a) If <m>c&gt;1</m>, you stretch <m>\mathbf{v}</m> to a length of
      <m>c|\mathbf{v}|</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      (b) If <m>0&lt;c&lt;1</m>, you compress <m>\mathbf{v}</m> to a length of
      <m>c|\mathbf{v}|</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      (c) If <m>c=-1</m>, you flip the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      (d) You can combine (c) with (a) and (b) to flip and stretch/compress at
      the same time.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Graph the vectors in example 1.7 by first graphing <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and
        then using the above note.
      </p>
      <!--\includegraphics[max width=0.5 \textwidth, left]{2025_08_01_3432b3c41241484aef56g&#x2D;08(1)}-->
      <!--\includegraphics[max width=0.5\textwidth, right]{2025_08_01_3432b3c41241484aef56g&#x2D;08}-->
      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We first graph <m>\mathbf{v}= \langle -1,1 \rangle</m>, which has length
        <m>|\mathbf{v}| = \sqrt{2}</m>. For <m>4\mathbf{v}</m>: since
        <m>c=4&gt;1</m>, we stretch <m>\mathbf{v}</m> to a length of
        <m>4|\mathbf{v}| = 4\sqrt{2}</m> keeping its direction, ending at
        <m>(-4,4)</m>. For <m>-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{v}</m>: combining (c) with (b)
        in the note above, we flip the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and
        compress it to a length of
        <m>\frac{1}{2}|\mathbf{v}| = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>, ending at
        <m>\left( \frac{1}{2},-\frac{1}{2}\right)</m>. Both graphs are shown in
        the figures below.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <sidebyside widths="45%  45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
<image source="/images/Example 1.8-1.png">  <shortdescription>The vector v with coordinates x = -1,y = 1 and the scaled vector 4v with coordinates x = -4, y = 4.</shortdescription>
      </image>
<image source="/images/Example 1.8-2.png">  <shortdescription>The vector v with coordinates x = -1,y = 1 and the scaled vector - 0.5v with coordinates x = 0.5, y = -0.5.</shortdescription>
      </image>



  </sidebyside>
  <definition>
    <title>Unit and Parallel Vectors</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A vector of length 1 is called a unit vector.
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a nonzero vector
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}\right\rangle</m>, the vector
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          \mathbf{u}=\frac{1}{|\mathbf{v}|}\mathbf{v}=\frac{\mathbf{v}}{|\mathbf{v}|}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        is a unit vector with the same direction as <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <m/>
      </p>

      <p>
        Two vectors <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> are parallel if they
        lie on the same line.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find a unit vector in the direction of
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 1,2\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p/>
      <p>
        Find all vectors of length 3 parallel to the vector
        <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle 1,2\rangle</m>.
      </p>

      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The magnitude of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
        <m>|\mathbf{v}| = \sqrt{1^{2}+2^{2}}= \sqrt{5}</m>, so a unit vector in
        the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{u}= \frac{\mathbf{v}}{|\mathbf{v}|}= \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}\langle 1,2 \rangle = \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt{5}}, \frac{2}{\sqrt{5}}\right\rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        Indeed,
        <m>|\mathbf{u}| = \sqrt{\frac{1}{5}+\frac{4}{5}}= \sqrt{1}= 1</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        A vector of length 3 parallel to <m>\mathbf{v}</m> must point either in
        the direction of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> or in the direction opposite of
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, so we scale the unit vector <m>\mathbf{u}</m> from
        the first part by <m>3</m> and by <m>-3</m>:
      </p>
      <md>
        3\mathbf{u}= \left\langle \frac{3}{\sqrt{5}}, \frac{6}{\sqrt{5}}\right\rangle \quad \text{and}\quad -3\mathbf{u}= \left\langle -\frac{3}{\sqrt{5}}, -\frac{6}{\sqrt{5}}\right\rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        These are the only two such vectors.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>


  <definition>
    <title>Components of a Vector</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a vector <m>\mathbf{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}\right\rangle</m>,
        we have the formulas
      </p>

      <p>
        <md>
          v_{1}=|\mathbf{v}| \cos (\theta) \quad \text{ and }\quad v_{2}=|\mathbf{v}| \sin (\theta),
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        where <m>\theta</m> is the angle giving the direction of
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, draw a picture summarizing the
        information in the definition above.
      </p>

      <image source="/images/Example 1.10.png">
       <shortdescription> A vector v with coordinates x =v1, y= v2 in standard position with the angle theta from the positive x-axis, showing v1 = magnitude of v times cos(theta) as the horizontal component and v2 = magnitude of v times sin(theta) as the vertical component.</shortdescription> 
      </image>          
    </statement>
  </example>



  <note>
    <p>
      You can represent a force by using a vector. In this context, the length
      of the vector is the magnitude of the force.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example>
    <title>Openstax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.1, Question 49</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Two forces, a horizontal force of 45 lb and another of 52 lb, act on the
        same object. The angle between these forces is <m>25^{\circ}</m>. Find
        the magnitude and direction angle from the positive x-axis of the
        resultant force that acts on the object. (Round to two decimal places.)
      </p>

      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We place the horizontal 45 lb force along the positive <m>x</m>-axis, so
        that <m>\mathbf{F}_{1} = \langle 45,0 \rangle</m>. The 52 lb force then
        makes an angle of <m>25^{\circ}</m> with the positive <m>x</m>-axis, so
        using the component formulas:
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{F}_{2} = \langle 52\cos(25^{\circ}), 52\sin(25^{\circ}) \rangle \approx \langle 47.128, 21.976 \rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        The resultant force is the sum of the two forces:
      </p>
      <md>
        \mathbf{F}= \mathbf{F}_{1}+\mathbf{F}_{2} \approx \langle 45+47.128, 0+21.976 \rangle = \langle 92.128, 21.976 \rangle
      </md>

      <p>
        Its magnitude is
      </p>
      <md>
        |\mathbf{F}| \approx \sqrt{92.128^{2}+21.976^{2}}= \sqrt{8487.568+482.945}= \sqrt{8970.513}\approx 94.71 \text{ lb}
      </md>

      <p>
        Since both components of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> are positive, the resultant
        lies in the first quadrant, so its direction angle <m>\theta</m> from
        the positive <m>x</m>-axis satisfies
      </p>
      <md>
        \tan(\theta) \approx \frac{21.976}{92.128}\Rightarrow \theta \approx \arctan(0.2385) \approx 13.42^{\circ}
      </md>
    </solution>
  </example>


  <remark>
    <p>
      The net force acting on an object is the sum of all individual forces
      acting on it.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <title>Openstax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.1, Question 57</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A 1500-lb boat is parked on a ramp that makes an angle of
        <m>30^{\circ}</m> with the horizontal. The boat's weight vector points
        downward and is a sum of two vectors: a vector <m>\mathbf{v}_{1}</m>
        that is parallel to the ramp and a vector <m>\mathbf{v}_{2}</m> that is
        perpendicular to the inclined surface. The magnitudes of vectors
        <m>\mathbf{v}_{1}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_{2}</m> are the components of
        the boat's weight vector parallel and perpendicular to the ramp,
        respectively. Find the magnitudes of <m>\mathbf{v}_{1}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{v}_{2}</m>. (Round to the nearest integer.)
      </p>

      <image source="/images/08ef1dafa6bfde7460b7a5d7416128ad6e67d50b.png">
       <shortdescription> A boat on a ramp inclined at 30 degrees, with the weight vector pointing downward decomposed into v1 parallel to the ramp and v2 perpendicular to the ramp.</shortdescription>
       <description>    Openstax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.1, Question 57</description>
      </image>        

      <p/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The weight vector <m>\mathbf{w}</m> points straight down and has
        magnitude <m>|\mathbf{w}| = 1500</m> lb. Since the ramp makes an angle
        of <m>30^{\circ}</m> with the horizontal, the direction perpendicular to
        the ramp makes that same angle of <m>30^{\circ}</m> with the vertical;
        that is, the angle between <m>\mathbf{w}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_{2}</m>
        is <m>30^{\circ}</m>, as in the picture above. Decomposing
        <m>\mathbf{w}</m> into these two perpendicular components:
      </p>
      <md>
        |\mathbf{v}_{1}| = 1500\sin(30^{\circ}) = 1500 \cdot \frac{1}{2}= 750 \text{ lb}\\  |\mathbf{v}_{2}| = 1500\cos(30^{\circ}) = 1500 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}= 750 \sqrt{3} \text{ lb}
      </md>

      <p>
        As a check, <m>\sqrt{750^{2}+1299^{2}}\approx 1500</m>, the magnitude of
        the weight vector.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>